@startuml neural-mmr
' Maximal Marginal Relevance as the summarizer instantiates it: the "query" of the
' original MMR formulation is replaced by the document centroid, so relevance means
' centrality. Greedy argmax with a redundancy penalty against the already-selected set.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/neural-mmr.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 1 in docs/components/neural/summarizer.md §4.
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam rectangle {
  FontColor white
  FontStyle bold
  BorderColor #263238
  roundCorner 12
}
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
title Maximal Marginal Relevance — centrality **minus** redundancy  (λ = 1 − diversity_threshold)

rectangle "**Candidate set R**\nsentence vectors v₁ … vₙ\n(ℓ₂-normalized, 768-dim)" as cand #2E7D32
rectangle "**Centroid c**\nc = v̄ / ‖v̄‖,   v̄ = (1/n) Σᵢ vᵢ\n//stands in for MMR's query//" as centroid #1565C0
rectangle "**Relevance**\nrel(sᵢ) = cos(vᵢ, c)\n“how central is this sentence?”" as rel #1565C0
rectangle "**Redundancy**\nred(sᵢ) = max cos(vᵢ, vⱼ)\n           sⱼ ∈ S\n“how much does it repeat S?”" as red #EF6C00
rectangle "**MMR score**\nλ·rel(sᵢ) − (1 − λ)·red(sᵢ)" as mmr #2E7D32
rectangle "**Selected set S**\nseeded with argmax rel\nthen grown greedily" as sel #6A1B9A
rectangle "**Summary**\nnum_sentences picks;\nre-ordered by original\nposition if preserve_order" as out #6A1B9A

cand --> centroid : mean, then normalize
centroid --> rel
cand --> rel
cand --> red
sel --> red : compare against\neach chosen sentence
rel --> mmr : weight λ
red --> mmr : weight −(1 − λ)
mmr --> sel : argmax over R ∖ S
sel --> mmr : loop until |S| = num_sentences
sel --> out

note bottom of mmr #E8F5E9
  λ = 1: pure centrality — the summary may say the same thing three times.
  λ = 0: pure novelty — the summary drifts to outliers.
  Default diversity_threshold = 0.3 ⟹ **λ = 0.7**.
  The first pick has S = ∅, so it is chosen on relevance alone; the code
  seeds S with argmax rel before the loop, which makes the max over S
  well-defined at every later step.
end note

@enduml
